  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery shows a trough ridge trough pattern \neast to west across the central CONUS. The sinusoidal nature of the \noverall flow is a broad contributor to the rollercoaster ride of \ntemperatures that are expected through the period. Northerly flow \ncontinues to dominate the short term forecast with cool Canadian air \nadvecting into the region. High temperatures only look to reach the \nupper 40s across the area. Points near La Cygne and Butler may touch \n50F. As the surface high continues to sag southwards, winds look to \nkeep conditions a bit brisk, but nowhere near the extend we saw the \npast 2 days (although temperatures were much warmer).\n\nThe peak of this cool stretch comes tonight into Saturday morning as \nthe center of the surface high slowly migrated through southern IA. \nLight surface winds as well as a combination of cold air advection \naloft (limiting an inversion) and clear skies allow strong nocturnal \ncooling which lowers overnight temperatures to the 20s with at 20-30 \npercent chance that portions of central and northeast MO could dip \ninto the upper teens.\n\nSub freezing lows continue through much of the weekend with portions \nof central and eastern MO seeing lows < 32F through Tuesday morning. \nLate in the weekend, the trough currently situated across the \nwestern CONUS begins to migrate eastward. As it does so, a lee \ntrough at low levels advects some warmer air from the southern CONUS \nnorthward pushing temperatures back upward to the 50s. Midweek a \nsubsequent trough and preceding ridge enter the central CONUS. Flow \nopens up from the desert SW and the Gulf which when combined with \nthe tighten pressure gradient between these systems vaults warm air \nnorthward. The result is another period of warmer than seasonal \ntemperatures with highs for the tail end of next week reaching the \n60s. If we flip the parameters of the forecast temperature ensembles \naround they show a 10-20 percent chance of temperatures exceeding \n70F which may not be a lot, but is more than one would expect this \nlate into November.\n\nThe warm temperatures next week are welcome as that looks to be the \nnext opportunity for precipitation across the region. Extended \nguidance hints at a fairly interesting situation developing \nparticularly in the midlevel where an expansive high across the \neastern CONUS and a low across the west create a tight pressure \ngradient across the intermountain west. This compression of warm air \nand moisture flow combined with the exit region of an upper level \ntrough shows the potential for the development of and expansive area \nof precipitation across the central CONUS Friday. However, much of \nthe activity looks to remain west of our area. Additional ejecting \nshortwaves from the trough through next weekend bring opportunities \nfor showers and maybe a few thunderstorms. Of course, several \nuncertainties remain and guidance does tend to spatially broad brush \nPoPs the further out in time they run. We will have to see how the \npattern evolves over the next several days.",
